 2. 🌏 Global tightening on tap 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 It's not just the Fed. Global monetary policy is abruptly swinging back to tightening mode, with some central banks facing tests to their credibility.

 Driving the news: The European Central Bank raised rates last week, and the Fed may follow suit, as we note above. 

 🇯🇵 The Bank of Japan is widely expected to raise interest rates on Friday, accelerating a tightening campaign that began in 2024. 🇬🇧 The Bank of England is expected to hold rates steady Thursday, but the focus will be on what governor Andrew Bailey signals about further hikes as inflation pressures build. The intrigue: The BOJ is being pulled in opposite directions as persistent inflation strengthens the case for higher rates.

 Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent has pressed Japan to raise rates after the U.S. intervened to prop up the yen this summer. He has warned traders against betting on renewed weakness, hinting that Washington knew tighter policy was coming: "I am the house now." On the other side is Japanese Prime Minister Sanae Takaichi, who favors easy monetary policy (though her administration appears accepting of the need for further hikes).

 Higher rates could help contain inflation and support the yen, though that would also raise borrowing costs and make it harder for Takaichi to deliver on her economic agenda. What to watch: Higher rates — signals that central banks are prepared to do more — could paradoxically help chill the surge in global borrowing costs if financial markets are convinced inflation will be contained.

 Of note: The European Central Bank's renewed inflation fight may soon come alongside a change at the top.

 A six-byline scoop by the Financial Times shows that a multinational horse-trading is underway over who will replace ECB president Christine Lagarde when she departs, as is widely expected, in the months ahead. National rivalries are complicating who will get the job. European officials hope to strike a "grand package" by year-end, balancing national claims across the ECB presidency, chief economist and another executive board seat, the FT reports. For example, Berlin sees a non-German president paired with a powerful German chief economist as one possible deal. 
 
